;; DEFERRED (split off from simple-error-print-object-method.issue).
;;
;; DEFINE-CONDITION (and DEFCLASS) cannot inherit from a *kernel* condition
;; class.  E.g.:
;;
;;   (define-condition terrible-error (simple-error) ())
;;   => Unhandled SIMPLE-ERROR: DEFCLASS ~S: superclass ~S not found
;;
;; DEFCLASS resolves superclasses only against the CLOS *%classes* registry, but
;; ERROR / SIMPLE-ERROR / SIMPLE-CONDITION / WARNING etc. are kernel classes (not
;; %cls objects), so they are "not found".  To support user condition types that
;; inherit the standard condition hierarchy, DEFCLASS superclass resolution must
;; fall back to the kernel class registry (and the CLOS class must bridge to the
;; kernel CPL so TYPEP / method dispatch keep working).
;;
;; Note: printing of simple-conditions/simple-errors and PRINT-OBJECT methods on
;; condition classes now work (see issues/closed/simple-error-print-object-method).

----------------------------------------------------------------------
RESOLVED (verified slice 52, v1.1.9) — moved to issues/closed.
This now works, courtesy of the CLOS/kernel bridge added since the issue was
filed: kernel condition classes are registered as %cls objects in *%classes*
(so FIND-CLASS / DEFCLASS superclass resolution find them) and the C-level
%clos-register teaches the kernel's class_of/subclassp to walk a CLOS class's
CPL — so a CLOS class that inherits a kernel condition class bridges to the
kernel CPL for TYPEP and HANDLER-CASE dispatch.

Verified:
  (define-condition terrible-error (simple-error) ())            ; defines cleanly
  (typep (make-condition 'terrible-error) 'error)      => T
  (typep (make-condition 'terrible-error) 'simple-error) => T
  (handler-case (error 'terrible-error ...) (simple-error (e) ...))  ; catches
  (handler-case (error 'terrible-error)     (error (e) ...))        ; catches
  (define-condition my-err (simple-error) ((code :initarg :code :reader my-err-code)))
    -> (my-err-code (make-condition 'my-err :code 42)) => 42
  (defclass plain-sub (error) ((tag :initarg :tag :accessor tag-of)))
    -> make-instance + accessor + (typep inst 'error) => T
